The last objection to constructor injection was always boilerplate — a service with six dependencies means a six-parameter constructor with six field assignments. Lombok's `@RequiredArgsConstructor` eliminates this entirely, and it's now the dominant pattern in production Spring Boot codebases.

```java
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class OrderService {
    private final UserRepository userRepository;
    private final PaymentGateway paymentGateway;
    private final InventoryService inventoryService;
    private final NotificationService notificationService;
}
```

Lombok generates the constructor at compile time. Since there's only one constructor, Spring injects without needing `@Autowired`. The test writes itself naturally — no Spring context, no reflection, no `@InjectMocks` magic.

```java
class OrderServiceTest {
    @BeforeEach
    void setUp() {
        service = new OrderService(
            mock(UserRepository.class),
            mock(PaymentGateway.class),
            mock(InventoryService.class),
            mock(NotificationService.class)
        );
    }
}
```

If someone adds a dependency to `OrderService`, this test fails to compile — forcing the test to be updated. With field injection, the new dependency would silently be `null`, causing a NullPointerException somewhere deeper in the test run. One signal that a class has too many dependencies: when the constructor exceeds five or six parameters, it's not a constructor problem — it's a **Single Responsibility Problem**. The constructor is telling you the class does too much.

The cleanest production pattern uses both annotations together: mark one bean as `@Primary` for the common case, and use `@Qualifier` only at injection points that genuinely need the non-default implementation.

```java
@Configuration
public class NotificationConfig {
    @Bean
    @Primary
    public NotificationService emailService() {
        return new EmailNotificationService();
    }

    @Bean
    public NotificationService smsService() {
        return new SmsNotificationService();
    }
}
```

Most services that depend on `NotificationService` get the email implementation automatically — no annotation clutter needed. Only the specific components that require SMS use `@Qualifier`.

```java
@Service
public class AlertService {
    private final NotificationService primaryNotifier;
    private final NotificationService fallbackNotifier;

    public AlertService(
            NotificationService primaryNotifier,
            @Qualifier("smsService") NotificationService fallbackNotifier) {
        this.primaryNotifier = primaryNotifier;
        this.fallbackNotifier = fallbackNotifier;
    }
}
```

Notice how `primaryNotifier` doesn't need any annotation — `@Primary` handles it. Only the fallback explicitly qualifies. This keeps most of the codebase clean while preserving precision where it matters.

For larger codebases, consider **custom qualifier annotations** instead of string-based names. Strings are fragile — rename the `@Bean` method and every `@Qualifier("oldName")` silently breaks at startup. A custom annotation like `@SmsChannel` is refactoring-safe and communicates intent better than a bean name ever could. If you find yourself creating more than three implementations of the same interface, a `Map<String, NotificationService>` injection — where Spring injects all implementations keyed by bean name — or a factory method is usually a better fit than scattering `@Qualifier` annotations.

The pattern is almost always two services that grew intertwined over time. One developer added a dependency in one direction, another developer added the reverse dependency in a separate PR, and neither noticed the cycle because they were working on different features.

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final InventoryService inventoryService;

    public OrderService(InventoryService inventoryService) {
        this.inventoryService = inventoryService;
    }

    public void placeOrder(Order order) {
        inventoryService.reserve(order.getItems());
    }
}

@Service
public class InventoryService {
    private final OrderService orderService;

    public InventoryService(OrderService orderService) {
        this.orderService = orderService;
    }

    public void onStockReplenished(String sku) {
        orderService.retryBackorders(sku);
    }
}
```

Spring starts creating `OrderService`, needs `InventoryService`, starts creating `InventoryService`, needs `OrderService` — which is already mid-construction. Deadlock. The application fails at startup with `BeanCurrentlyInCreationException`.

**Option 1 — Extract to a third service (best for most cases):**

Pull the cross-cutting logic into its own bean. `InventoryService` no longer depends on `OrderService` because the backorder retry responsibility lives in a dedicated class.

```java
@Service
public class BackorderService {
    private final OrderService orderService;

    public BackorderService(OrderService orderService) {
        this.orderService = orderService;
    }

    public void retryBackorders(String sku) {
        orderService.retryBackorders(sku);
    }
}
```

`InventoryService` now calls `BackorderService` instead of `OrderService`. The cycle is gone because you separated the "retry backorders" concern from core inventory logic.

**Option 2 — Use Spring events (best for decoupling):**

Events eliminate the dependency entirely. `InventoryService` publishes a fact without knowing or caring who listens.

```java
@Service
public class InventoryService {
    private final ApplicationEventPublisher events;

    public void onStockReplenished(String sku) {
        events.publishEvent(new StockReplenishedEvent(sku));
    }
}

@Component
public class BackorderListener {
    private final OrderService orderService;

    @EventListener
    public void handle(StockReplenishedEvent event) {
        orderService.retryBackorders(event.getSku());
    }
}
```

This completely decouples inventory from order logic. The event-driven approach scales better as more consumers need stock replenishment notifications.

**Option 3 — @Lazy (workaround only):**

`@Lazy` injects a proxy instead of the real bean, deferring construction to first method call. The cycle still exists in your design — you've just delayed when it matters. Use this only as a bridge while you plan the real refactor.

```java
public OrderService(@Lazy InventoryService inventoryService) {
    this.inventoryService = inventoryService;
}
```

The most subtle scope bug happens when you inject a prototype bean into a singleton. You expect a fresh instance every time you use it, but the injection happens **once** — at singleton construction time. The singleton holds the same prototype instance forever, defeating the purpose entirely.

```java
@Component
@Scope("prototype")
public class RequestTracker {
    private final List<String> events = new ArrayList<>();
    public void track(String event) { events.add(event); }
}

@Service
public class AuditService {
    private final RequestTracker tracker;  // Injected once, never refreshed

    public AuditService(RequestTracker tracker) {
        this.tracker = tracker;
    }
}
```

`AuditService` gets one `RequestTracker` at startup and reuses that same instance for every request. Events accumulate across requests, and concurrent threads mutate the shared list without synchronization.

The fix is `ObjectProvider` — Spring's type-safe way to fetch a fresh prototype on demand instead of holding a stale reference.

```java
@Service
public class AuditService {
    private final ObjectProvider<RequestTracker> trackerProvider;

    public AuditService(ObjectProvider<RequestTracker> trackerProvider) {
        this.trackerProvider = trackerProvider;
    }

    public void audit(String event) {
        RequestTracker tracker = trackerProvider.getObject();  // Fresh instance
        tracker.track(event);
    }
}
```

Each call to `getObject()` returns a new prototype instance, preserving the isolation you expected. In practice, 99% of beans should be singletons. Reach for prototype only when you genuinely need per-use state isolation — report generators, request-scoped builders, or temporary computation objects.

The `${property.key}` syntax resolves against Spring's `Environment`, which aggregates properties from `application.properties`, environment variables, system properties, and command-line arguments. Spring walks these sources in priority order and returns the first match.

Resolution happens at **bean creation time**, not at method call time. The value is injected once and cached in the field. If the property changes in the file after startup, the bean still holds the old value — there's no live reload unless you use Spring Cloud Config's `@RefreshScope`.

```java
@Service
public class PaymentClient {
    @Value("${payment.api.url}")
    private String apiUrl;

    @Value("${payment.timeout:5000}")
    private int timeoutMs;

    @Value("${payment.retries:3}")
    private int maxRetries;
}
```

The colon acts as a separator between the key and the default value. Watch for a subtle trap: there's **no space after the colon**. Writing `${key: default}` injects `" default"` with a leading space, which causes silent bugs in URLs and numeric parsing. Always use `${key:default}` without spaces.

While `${}` performs simple property lookup, `#{}` evaluates a **Spring Expression Language** expression. SpEL can call methods, access system properties, reference other beans, and perform arithmetic — which makes it powerful but also harder to debug when something goes wrong.

```java
@Value("#{systemProperties['java.version']}")
private String javaVersion;

@Value("#{T(Runtime).getRuntime().availableProcessors()}")
private int cpuCores;

@Value("#{'${allowed.origins}'.split(',')}")
private List<String> allowedOrigins;
```

The combined syntax in the third example — `#{'${key}'.split(',')}` — first resolves the property placeholder, then applies SpEL to the result. This is the standard pattern for converting a comma-separated property into a `List`. Note the nesting: `${}` is inside `#{}`, never the reverse.

SpEL in `@Value` is evaluated at startup. If the expression fails — a null property, a class not found, a method that doesn't exist — you get a `SpelEvaluationException` and the application won't start. Keep expressions simple. If you need complex logic, move it to a `@PostConstruct` method where you can handle errors gracefully and log meaningful diagnostics.

The moment you have three or more `@Value` fields sharing a prefix — `${mail.host}`, `${mail.port}`, `${mail.username}` — you should migrate to `@ConfigurationProperties`. The benefits compound as the configuration group grows.

`@ConfigurationProperties` binds an entire prefix to a POJO. Fields map to properties automatically. Nested objects, lists, and maps work without SpEL. Most importantly, you can add `@Validated` and use **JSR-303 annotations** — `@NotBlank`, `@Min(1)`, `@Max(65535)` — to validate at startup. A missing required property fails with a clear message listing exactly what's wrong, instead of a generic `IllegalArgumentException`.

```java
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "mail")
@Validated
public class MailProperties {
    @NotBlank private String host;
    @Min(1) @Max(65535) private int port = 587;
    private Duration connectionTimeout = Duration.ofSeconds(30);
}
```

Spring parses `Duration` values like `20s`, `5m`, or `PT30S` automatically — no conversion code needed. Spring Boot's annotation processor generates `spring-configuration-metadata.json` from `@ConfigurationProperties` classes, giving IDEs auto-completion for your custom properties. Your team stops guessing property names — the IDE tells them. This alone justifies the migration for any property group used by more than one developer.

The most common `@Autowired` failure is `NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException` — and it typically appears when someone adds a new bean of an already-registered type, breaking injection points that previously had only one candidate.

```java
@Bean
public DataSource primaryDb() {
    return new HikariDataSource(primaryConfig);
}

@Bean
public DataSource replicaDb() {
    return new HikariDataSource(replicaConfig);
}
```

Before `replicaDb` existed, every `@Autowired DataSource` in the application resolved cleanly. After adding it, Spring finds two candidates and every unqualified injection point fails. The fix maps directly to the resolution algorithm: add `@Primary` to the original bean so it wins by default, and use `@Qualifier` only where you need the replica.

```java
@Bean
@Primary
public DataSource primaryDb() {
    return new HikariDataSource(primaryConfig);
}
```

The fix takes thirty seconds once you understand the resolution algorithm. Without understanding it, you'll spend thirty minutes reading Spring's error output and guessing. The algorithm — **type → qualifier → primary → name → failure** — is your debugging checklist.

Consider a payment integration with five properties scattered across three services via `@Value`. Each service parses values independently, none validates, and a missing property causes a runtime failure deep in a business flow. The `@ConfigurationProperties` migration centralizes everything into one class that validates at startup.

```java
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "payment")
@Validated
public class PaymentProperties {
    @NotBlank private String apiUrl;
    @NotBlank private String apiKey;
    @Min(100) @Max(30000) private int timeoutMs = 5000;
    private int maxRetries = 3;
    private Duration circuitBreakerTimeout = Duration.ofSeconds(10);
    // Getters and setters
}
```

Every service that needs payment configuration now injects this single class instead of multiple `@Value` strings. If `payment.api-url` is missing, the application fails at startup with: `Binding validation errors on payment: apiUrl must not be blank`. No ambiguity, no guessing, no runtime surprise.

Register the class with `@EnableConfigurationProperties(PaymentProperties.class)` on any `@Configuration` class, or simply add `@Component` to the properties class itself. Spring Boot's annotation processor generates `spring-configuration-metadata.json` from these classes, giving IDEs auto-completion for your custom properties in `application.properties` and `application.yml`. Your team stops guessing property names — the IDE tells them.

The safe production pattern is selective laziness with explicit warmup. Mark specific heavy beans as `@Lazy` — report generators, ML model loaders, rarely-used admin services — and then verify them immediately after startup using an `ApplicationReadyEvent` listener.

```java
@Service
@Lazy
public class ReportGenerator {
    private final List<Template> templates;

    public ReportGenerator(TemplateRepository repo) {
        this.templates = repo.loadAll();  // Expensive: 8 seconds
    }
}
```

The `ReportGenerator` doesn't block startup, but you don't want to discover it's broken on the first real report request. A warmup listener forces construction immediately after startup, while the application is still in its "warming up" phase before receiving real traffic.

```java
@Component
public class StartupWarmup {
    private final ObjectProvider<ReportGenerator> reportGenerator;

    public StartupWarmup(ObjectProvider<ReportGenerator> reportGenerator) {
        this.reportGenerator = reportGenerator;
    }

    @EventListener(ApplicationReadyEvent.class)
    public void warmup() {
        reportGenerator.getObject();  // Force creation, catch failures early
        log.info("Report generator warmed up successfully");
    }
}
```

If `ReportGenerator` construction fails, the error appears in logs immediately after startup — not on the first user request. The application is technically "ready" (health check passed), but the warmup failure is logged and can trigger an alert. This gives you the startup speed benefit without silently deferring failures to user-facing requests.

The anti-pattern is `spring.main.lazy-initialization=true` in production without any warmup. You save 15 seconds on startup and spend 15 minutes debugging why the first request after deployment fails intermittently — because the failure depends on which lazy bean gets touched first by which request path.

`@ConditionalOnProperty` is the most practically useful conditional annotation for application developers. It registers a bean only when a property has a specific value, giving you a property-driven feature flag system with zero custom code.

```java
@Bean
@ConditionalOnProperty(
    name = "feature.notifications.enabled",
    havingValue = "true",
    matchIfMissing = false
)
public NotificationService notificationService() {
    return new SmtpNotificationService();
}
```

The `matchIfMissing` parameter is where most developers make mistakes. Set it to `true` and the bean is created when the property doesn't exist at all — effectively making the feature **opt-out** instead of opt-in. For production features, `matchIfMissing = false` is safer: the feature is off by default, explicitly enabled per environment.

Toggle a feature between environments by setting `feature.notifications.enabled=true` in staging's `application-staging.properties` and omitting it (or setting `false`) in production. No code changes, no conditional `if` blocks scattered across services, no redeployment for a toggle flip.

`@ConditionalOnClass` checks whether a Java class exists on the classpath at startup. This is the foundation of Spring Boot's "add a dependency, get a configuration" experience — and it works because Maven/Gradle dependencies determine which classes are available at runtime.

```java
@Configuration
@ConditionalOnClass(RedisOperations.class)
@EnableConfigurationProperties(RedisProperties.class)
public class RedisAutoConfiguration {
    @Bean
    @ConditionalOnMissingBean
    public RedisTemplate<String, Object> redisTemplate(RedisConnectionFactory factory) {
        RedisTemplate<String, Object> template = new RedisTemplate<>();
        template.setConnectionFactory(factory);
        return template;
    }
}
```

Notice the **two-level conditioning**. `@ConditionalOnClass` on the class gates the entire configuration — if the Redis library isn't on the classpath, none of the `@Bean` methods are even evaluated. `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` on the individual bean method allows you to override that specific bean by declaring your own.

When a class-level condition fails, Spring skips the entire configuration without error. This is more efficient than putting conditions on individual methods, because the class-level condition prevents Spring from even parsing the method-level bean definitions. Every Spring Boot auto-configuration class follows this pattern.

`@ConditionalOnMissingBean` is how Spring Boot implements "sensible defaults you can replace." It checks the bean registry during context refresh and only creates its bean if no other bean of that type is already registered.

The critical detail is **evaluation order**. User-defined `@Configuration` classes are processed before auto-configuration classes. This means your `@Bean DataSource customDataSource()` is registered first, and when `DataSourceAutoConfiguration` evaluates `@ConditionalOnMissingBean(DataSource.class)`, it finds your bean and backs off. The ordering is deliberate — `@AutoConfiguration` classes are explicitly loaded after regular configuration classes.

```java
@Configuration
public class DatabaseConfig {
    @Bean
    public DataSource dataSource() {
        return new HikariDataSource(customSettings());
    }
}
// Spring Boot auto-config evaluates @ConditionalOnMissingBean(DataSource.class)
// → finds your bean → skips its own DataSource creation
```

If your custom bean isn't being picked up and the auto-configured one wins, check that your `@Configuration` class is actually being component-scanned. A `@Configuration` in a package outside `@ComponentScan`'s reach is invisible to Spring — your bean is never registered, the condition passes, and the auto-configured default takes over. To debug any conditional evaluation, set `debug=true` in your properties or hit `/actuator/conditions` — Spring Boot prints a detailed report of every condition that matched or failed and why.

The most common scanning failure is a class annotated with `@Service` that Spring never detects. The symptom is a `NoSuchBeanDefinitionException` for a class you can clearly see in your source code. The cause is almost always a package boundary issue.

```java
// Main class in com.example.app
@SpringBootApplication  // Scans com.example.app.**
public class Application { }

// This service IS found (under com.example.app)
// com.example.app.service.UserService
@Service
public class UserService { }

// This service is NOT found (different root package)
// com.example.shared.AuthService
@Service
public class AuthService { }
```

The fix is explicit base package declaration — either by adding `@ComponentScan` with additional packages, or by restructuring the project to keep everything under a common root.

```java
@SpringBootApplication
@ComponentScan(basePackages = {"com.example.app", "com.example.shared"})
public class Application { }
```

A safer alternative is `basePackageClasses`, which uses a marker class to identify the package. String-based package names are refactoring-fragile — rename the package and the scan silently stops working. `basePackageClasses = SharedConfig.class` moves with the class during refactoring.

One production trap worth knowing: placing your main class in the **default package** (no `package` declaration) triggers a scan of the entire classpath — every class in every JAR. Startup takes minutes, unpredictable beans get registered, and you'll see errors from third-party library classes being treated as Spring components. Always declare a proper package for your main class.
